{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-213483",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14269988/assets/b65fc4830e0b060d62226b41d5ccb7bdc63d0ad594f7a42a2628597409bba3af.png)\n\n      \n\nRecall the following facts regarding the congruence $ax≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n):$\n\n- The congruence has no solutions modulo $n$ if and only if \n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)\\,\\text{⧸}|\\,b.\n$$\n\n- The congruence has $\\text{gcd}(a,n)$ solutions modulo $n$ if and only if \n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)\\,|\\,b.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our congruences.\n\n- Congruence I has three solutions modulo $21.$ We have $a=9$ and $n=21$, and so\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(9,21)=3.\n$$\n\nSince $b=12$ is divisible by $3,$ this congruence has $3$ solutions.\n\n- Congruence II has three solutions modulo $21.$ We have $a=6$ and $n=21$, and so\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(6,21)=3.\n$$\n\nSince $b=15$ is divisible by $3,$ this congruence has $3$ solutions.\n\n- Congruence III has no solutions. We have $a=12$ and $n=21$, and so\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(12,21)=3.\n$$\n\nSince $b=8$ is not divisible by $3,$ this congruence has no solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\".",
